#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/id #uuid "4f2ec8a1-0ab4-55ea-9472-2dd7b9fa5f8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13151" :question/problem "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\}$, $B = \\{f,g\\}$, and $C = A \\times B$, determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n1. $(f,4) \\in C$\n2. $(7,g) \\in C$\n3. $(3,f) \\in C$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/488cc12c3ff8d8b797a5ecd57846bd45.png)\n\nHence, the set\n\n$$\nC = A \\times B\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{\\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "0a101406-0053-5507-8989-3c1cd6e8d4e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13152" :question/problem "If $A = \\{p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{4,9 \\}$, which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B$?\n\n1. $((p,4),9)$\n2. $((q,9),4)$\n3. $(p,4,9)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{p,q \\}$ with $\\{4,9 \\}$, as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/41f76e800a83fdbf548fb4a281d338f4.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{(p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A \\times B & = & A & \\cdot & B\\end{vmatrix} = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n$(A \\times B) \\times B$\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B$, as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/12cd1413549bdde65ed735cf3e17b99f.png)\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}(A \\times B) \\times B & = & A \\times B & \\cdot & B\\end{vmatrix} = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88fb08f1-03f1-5b04-8b48-4b153e522040" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Cartesian Product"}] :question/id #uuid "677fc67f-94df-57a7-a804-22337acda702" :question/math-academy-id "e-13154" :question/problem "If $C = \\{3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D =\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\}$, what is the cardinality of $C \\times D$?" :question/worked-solution "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D$, the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}C \\times D & = & C & \\cdot & D\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}C \\cdot D & &= & C & \\cdot & D\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054351 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/id #uuid "2bc77b3d-fca6-5f66-ac12-e9fedcce15bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13160" :question/problem "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}$." :question/worked-solution "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA^2 = A \\times A\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/144ff19e358a073bcea04a9719793455.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/49/Source/Images/4f01eda6f1609be0d129be13ac2d565c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a3417cd-8ada-5236-afd5-66a860cbea9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051402} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad2d185-1f78-5b45-830a-db287636c4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051403} {:answer/id #uuid "1be8576f-4e06-5606-8b37-6b1a1cd5f101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051404} {:answer/id #uuid "41599e7e-ea21-5302-9c02-fec4d20a4f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051405} {:answer/id #uuid "797f4fb9-d76d-5702-b11e-fd68a6488bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1be8576f-4e06-5606-8b37-6b1a1cd5f101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051404} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2931e1c-1e46-5be4-80cc-2c42fbe48a59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051401}] :question/id #uuid "c012134a-99e0-5f06-b534-c2d2ae072768" :question/math-academy-id "q-1257" :question/problem "For the sets $A = {2, 4, 6}$, $B = {x, y}$, and $C = A \\cdot B$, determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n1. $(2, 4) ∈ C$\n2. $(2, x) ∈ C$\n3. $(3, y) ∈ C$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88fb08f1-03f1-5b04-8b48-4b153e522040" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c855f86-9825-5ba0-8dc4-45eb65aa49eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051420} {:answer/id #uuid "ac80f671-442c-56aa-9fc2-c64bc282e18f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051421} {:answer/id #uuid "e403759a-a567-5a7d-b16e-9666f6492516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051422} {:answer/id #uuid "3f16caff-fb37-5427-8829-b57e90502110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051423} {:answer/id #uuid "587a087d-c724-59ca-a845-c2b38e37ad6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c855f86-9825-5ba0-8dc4-45eb65aa49eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051420} :answer-field/id #uuid "834fb79b-8881-57fd-b95e-72341351a53e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051419}] :question/id #uuid "0afacde2-053a-5abf-a5c4-ded995eb51ce" :question/ma